hey this is presh talwalkar  a marshmallow has the shape of a  cylinder  it has a diameter of 5 and a height of  3.  a dessert is made with 24 marshmallows  the bottom layer consists of three rows  of four marshmallows  a top layer consists of exactly the same  arrangement marshmallows are placed on  top of the bottom layer  the dessert is completed when chocolate  is poured to exactly fill the gaps  between the marshmallows  this is a side view of the dessert  here's a top view of the dessert  chocolate exactly fills the gaps between  the marshmallows  the question is what is the volume of  chocolate  this problem was asked to grade 12  students in australia  can you figure it out  give this problem a try and when you're  ready keep watching the video for the  solution  the main principle is that the volume of  the chocolate is equal to the area of a  cross section of chocolate times the  height of chocolate  the dessert consists of two layers of  marshmallows each which have a height of  three  the total height of the chocolate will  therefore be six  so the volume is equal to the area of a  single cross section times six  we can see the cross section from the  top view  this forms the shape of six different  gaps between the marshmallows  each of the six gaps are identical  we therefore can simplify this to say  the volume is equal to six times the  area of a single gap  multiplied by the height of six  so the volume is equal to 36 times the  area  of the single gap between four  marshmallows  what is that area  we start out by having 4 marshmallows  each marshmallow has a diameter of 5  which means each circle has a radius of  5 halves  we now draw a square that connects the  center of these four circles  the square side length consists of the  radius of two adjacent circles  the side length of the square is  therefore equal to the diameter of the  circle  so the side length is equal to five  the area between the gaps is equal to  the area of this square  minus the area of four quarter circles  so we can write this as the area of the  gap is equal to the area of the square  minus the area of four quarter circles  now four quarter circles together make  one whole circle  the area of the square is equal to s  squared and the area of the circle is  equal to pi r squared  this means the area of the square is  equal to 25 and the area of the circle  is equal to 25 pi divided by four  therefore the area of the gap is equal  to the difference between these two  areas  we can substitute this back into our  volume formula and then simplify to get  our answer that the volume is equal to  900 minus 225 pi which is approximately  193.1  did you figure it out  thanks for watching this video please  subscribe to my channel i make videos on  math and game theory you can catch me on  my blog mind your decisions which you  can follow on facebook google plus and  patreon you can catch me on social media  at preshtalwalker and if you like this  video please check out my books there  are links in the video description
 

